  "slug": "wiggle-subsequence",
  "title": "Wiggle Subsequence",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Dp 1d",
  "patterns": [
    "dynamic programming",
    "greedy"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "at each position, what actually matters isn't the exact value, just whether the most recent step (if any) went up or down",
    "keep two running counts: the longest wiggle subsequence ending with an up-step, and the longest ending with a down-step",
    "when the next number is greater than the previous one, it can extend any subsequence that last went down (turning it into one ending up)",
    "up = down + 1 when nums[i] > nums[i-1]; down = up + 1 when nums[i] < nums[i-1]; equal values change neither - the answer is max(up, down) at the end"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "trying to explicitly build subsequences instead of just tracking the two running lengths (ending-up and ending-down)",
    "incrementing both up and down on every step instead of only the one that a strict rise or fall actually extends",
    "not handling equal consecutive values, which should leave both running counts unchanged"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A sequence is called \"wiggly\" if consecutive differences strictly alternate between positive and negative (up, down, up, down, ... or down, up, down, up, ...); a sequence of fewer than two elements counts as wiggly by default. You're given a list of integers `nums`.\n\n**Find the length of the longest subsequence of `nums` that is wiggly, where elements may be skipped but their relative order kept.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "nums.length >= 1",
      "a wiggly sequence alternates strictly between rising and falling steps",
      "equal consecutive values break the alternation (neither rising nor falling)"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single element (length 1, trivially wiggly)",
      "all elements equal (best possible is length 1, since no step ever wiggles)",
      "a strictly monotonic sequence (best possible is length 2)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 7, 4, 9, 2, 5]",
        "output": "6",
        "expectedOutput": "6",
        "explanation": "the differences are +6, -3, +5, -7, +3 - already alternating, so the whole array qualifies"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]",
        "output": "2",
        "expectedOutput": "2",
        "explanation": "the sequence is strictly increasing, so no differences alternate; the best you can do is any 2 elements"
      }
    ],
